}
}
class Child extends Parent {
}
```

The Child class inherits the accessible methods of Parent. It can use these methods without defining them again.

Answer: Method overriding occurs when a child class provides its own implementation of an inherited instance method using the same method signature and a compatible return type. Example:

```java
class Parent {
```

void methodOne() {

```java
System.out.println("Hi");
}
}
class Child extends Parent {
@Override
```

void methodOne() {

```java
System.out.println("Hi, how are you?");
}
}
```

The child class changes the implementation of methodOne() while retaining the method's signature.

