Answer: Downcasting is the process of converting a reference of a parent type into a reference of a child type. Example:

```java
Parent ref = new Child();
Child c = (Child) ref;
c.methodFour();
```

Here, ref refers to a Child object. The cast (Child) ref converts the reference to the child type, allowing access to child-specific methods. Downcasting is valid only when the object referred to is compatible with the target child type. An incompatible cast causes a ClassCastException at runtime.

Answer: It is called downcasting because the reference type is converted from a parent type to a more specific child type in the inheritance hierarchy. For example:

```java
Parent ref = new Child();
Child c = (Child) ref;
```

The reference initially has the type Parent. It is then cast to Child, which is lower in the inheritance hierarchy.

