Answer: Upcasting Downcasting Uses a parent-type reference for a child object. Converts a parent-type reference to a child-type reference. Usually happens implicitly. Requires an explicit cast. Supports access through the parent type. Allows access to child-specific members after a valid cast.

```java
Example: Parent ref = new Child();
Example: Child c = (Child) ref;
```

Upcasting is commonly used to achieve loose coupling. Downcasting is useful when child-specific functionality is required and the referenced object is compatible with that child type.

