```

Both approaches make ref refer to a Child object through the Parent type. The single-statement version is concise and commonly used.

Answer: When an object reference is passed to a method, Java passes the value of that reference. The receiving parameter can then refer to the same object. Example:

```java
class Parent {
```

void display() {

```java
System.out.println("Parent method");
}
}
class Child extends Parent {
@Override
```

void display() {

```java
System.out.println("Child method");
}
}
public class Launch {
static void show(Parent ref) {
ref.display();
}
public static void main(String[] args) {
Child c = new Child();
show(c);
}
}
```

Output: Child method The parameter ref has the type Parent, but it refers to a Child object. Therefore, the overridden method in Child executes.

Answer: When a method accepts a parent-type parameter, it can work with multiple child objects without requiring a separate method for every child class. For example:

```java
class Parent {
```

void display() {

```java
System.out.println("Parent");
}
}
class Dog extends Parent {
@Override
```

void display() {

```java
System.out.println("Dog");
}
}
class Cat extends Parent {
@Override
```

void display() {

```java
System.out.println("Cat");
}
}
class Parrot extends Parent {
@Override
```

void display() {

```java
System.out.println("Parrot");
}
}
public class Launch {
static void show(Parent ref) {
ref.display();
}
public static void main(String[] args) {
show(new Dog());
show(new Cat());
show(new Parrot());
}
}
```

Output: Dog Cat Parrot The same show() method handles all three object types. Without this approach, separate methods could be written for Dog, Cat, and Parrot.

Answer: Runtime polymorphism occurs when an overridden instance method is selected according to the actual object's type at runtime. Example:

```java
Parent ref = new Child();
ref.methodOne();
```

Although the reference type is Parent, the object is of type Child. If Child overrides methodOne(), the child's implementation executes. The reference type determines which members can be accessed at compile time, while the actual object's type determines which overridden instance method implementation executes at runtime.

Answer: Method overloading means defining multiple methods with the same name in the same class but with different parameter lists. The parameter lists can differ in: Number of parameters. Types of parameters. Order of parameter types. Example:

```java
class Calculator {
int add(int a, int b) {
return a + b;
}
int add(int a, int b, int c) {
return a + b + c;
}
double add(double a, double b) {
return a + b;
}
}
```

The three methods share the name add, but their parameter lists differ. The compiler selects the appropriate method based on the arguments supplied. Changing only the return type does not constitute method overloading.
